1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,240
Here are two clear, colourless solutions: potassium iodide

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:05,520
and lead nitrate.

3
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:07,520
Add a few drops of one to the

4
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:10,160
other, and a bright yellow solid appears and

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:11,360
slowly settles.

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Neither bottle had anything yellow in it.

7
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:16,320
So where did the solid come from?

8
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:18,400
And what's left in the liquid around it?

9
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:22,240
As you saw in the dissolving video, potassium

10
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,440
iodide solution isn't full of K I units.

11
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:29,760
It's potassium ions and iodide ions, each surrounded

12
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:31,600
by water, moving separately.

13
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:35,600
Lead nitrate solution is lead ions and nitrate

14
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:36,160
ions.

15
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:38,480
So when you mix them, you have four

16
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:40,480
kinds of ions in one tube.

17
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:42,880
The only new thing that can happen is

18
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:46,560
that ions meet new partners: lead with iodide,

19
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:48,320
or potassium with nitrate.

20
00:00:49,534 --> 00:00:52,653
Lead ions and iodide ions attract each other

21
00:00:52,734 --> 00:00:55,693
more strongly than water can keep them apart,

22
00:00:55,814 --> 00:00:57,853
so they lock into a solid lattice of

23
00:00:57,934 --> 00:01:00,813
lead iodide that comes out of the solution.

24
00:01:01,134 --> 00:01:02,973
A solid that forms like this is a

25
00:01:03,094 --> 00:01:04,013
precipitate.

26
00:01:04,334 --> 00:01:07,933
Potassium and nitrate ions stay dissolved, exactly as

27
00:01:08,014 --> 00:01:09,933
they were, so they take no part.

28
00:01:10,494 --> 00:01:13,133
Ions like that are called spectator ions.

29
00:01:13,854 --> 00:01:16,653
So after mixing, the tube holds solid lead

30
00:01:16,814 --> 00:01:20,333
iodide, with potassium ions and nitrate ions still

31
00:01:20,414 --> 00:01:21,613
dissolved in the water.

32
00:01:22,834 --> 00:01:24,833
So how could you know in advance which

33
00:01:24,914 --> 00:01:25,953
pair locks together?

34
00:01:26,434 --> 00:01:29,553
Chemists mixed pairs of solutions again and again

35
00:01:29,794 --> 00:01:32,273
and recorded which ones made a solid, and

36
00:01:32,354 --> 00:01:35,393
the patterns they found are the solubility rules,

37
00:01:35,474 --> 00:01:37,673
a first prediction from experiments.

38
00:01:37,954 --> 00:01:40,433
In the practical video, you'll find them yourself.

39
00:01:41,634 --> 00:01:46,993
Here's the version to learn, in order: NAGSAG.

40
00:01:47,314 --> 00:01:50,753
Nitrates, Ammonium, and Group 1 ions like sodium

41
00:01:50,834 --> 00:01:53,913
and potassium: almost always soluble.

42
00:01:54,194 --> 00:01:58,673
Then Sulfates: soluble, except with barium, strontium and

43
00:01:58,834 --> 00:02:02,193
lead, which are insoluble, and calcium and silver,

44
00:02:02,274 --> 00:02:04,513
which are only slightly soluble.

45
00:02:04,634 --> 00:02:06,593
Acetates: soluble.

46
00:02:06,714 --> 00:02:10,433
And Group 17, the halides chloride, bromide and

47
00:02:10,514 --> 00:02:14,273
iodide: soluble, except with silver and lead.

48
00:02:14,474 --> 00:02:17,873
Everything else, like carbonates and hydroxides, is mostly

49
00:02:18,034 --> 00:02:20,513
insoluble, unless it's paired with a Group 1

50
00:02:20,714 --> 00:02:22,273
ion or ammonium.

51
00:02:22,394 --> 00:02:26,033
Barium hydroxide is one more exception: it dissolves.

52
00:02:27,234 --> 00:02:28,553
So here's the method.

53
00:02:28,834 --> 00:02:32,753
List the four ions, then swap partners: each

54
00:02:32,834 --> 00:02:35,633
positive ion pairs with the other solution's negative

55
00:02:35,714 --> 00:02:36,313
ion.

56
00:02:36,594 --> 00:02:38,713
That gives two possible products.

57
00:02:38,994 --> 00:02:41,553
Then check both against the rules.

58
00:02:41,674 --> 00:02:44,593
If one is insoluble, that's your precipitate.

59
00:02:44,674 --> 00:02:46,993
If both are soluble, nothing forms.

60
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:51,720
Try the first syllabus pair: potassium chloride and

61
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:52,840
silver nitrate.

62
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:57,560
Swap partners: silver chloride and potassium nitrate.

63
00:02:58,040 --> 00:03:01,240
Potassium nitrate is a nitrate, and potassium is

64
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:03,320
Group 1, so it's soluble.

65
00:03:03,880 --> 00:03:06,840
Silver chloride is a halide with silver, one

66
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:09,560
of the exceptions, so it's insoluble.

67
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:11,640
A white precipitate forms.

68
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:15,720
The full equation is potassium chloride plus silver

69
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:20,120
nitrate gives silver chloride solid, plus potassium nitrate,

70
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:20,920
aqueous.

71
00:03:22,134 --> 00:03:25,173
That equation hides what's really in the tube.

72
00:03:25,414 --> 00:03:28,213
So write every aqueous compound as its separate

73
00:03:28,374 --> 00:03:28,933
ions.

74
00:03:29,414 --> 00:03:33,333
On the left: potassium ions, chloride ions, silver

75
00:03:33,414 --> 00:03:35,333
ions and nitrate ions.

76
00:03:35,894 --> 00:03:39,253
On the right, potassium nitrate splits too, but

77
00:03:39,334 --> 00:03:41,813
silver chloride is a solid, so it stays

78
00:03:41,894 --> 00:03:42,293
whole.

79
00:03:42,854 --> 00:03:45,173
This is the full ionic equation.

80
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:48,960
Now look for ions that appear unchanged on

81
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:50,040
both sides.

82
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:52,160
Potassium and nitrate.

83
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:54,880
They're the spectators, so cross them out.

84
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:58,080
What's left is the net ionic equation: silver

85
00:03:58,160 --> 00:04:02,000
ion plus chloride ion gives silver chloride solid.

86
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:04,800
Check the charges: plus one and minus one

87
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:07,040
make zero on the left, and the solid

88
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:08,080
is neutral.

89
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:10,560
And this net equation is the same wherever

90
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:12,880
the silver and chloride ions came from.

91
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:16,580
Swap partners for the hook's pair: lead with

92
00:04:16,740 --> 00:04:19,260
iodide, potassium with nitrate.

93
00:04:19,540 --> 00:04:21,620
Potassium nitrate is soluble.

94
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:24,260
Lead with a halide is an exception, so

95
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:27,060
lead iodide is the bright yellow precipitate.

96
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:30,020
But its formula isn't P b I.

97
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:32,500
A lead ion is two plus, and an

98
00:04:32,580 --> 00:04:35,300
iodide ion is only one minus, so it

99
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:38,500
takes two iodides to balance one lead: P

100
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:40,020
b I two.

101
00:04:40,340 --> 00:04:42,740
That's why the full equation needs two potassium

102
00:04:42,820 --> 00:04:45,700
iodides, and why two potassium ions and two

103
00:04:45,860 --> 00:04:47,980
nitrate ions are the spectators.

104
00:04:48,260 --> 00:04:50,820
The net ionic equation is lead ion plus

105
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:54,100
two iodide ions gives lead iodide solid.

106
00:04:54,580 --> 00:04:57,860
Charges: plus two, and two minus ones, make

107
00:04:57,940 --> 00:04:58,500
zero.

108
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:02,740
The third pair is sodium sulfate and barium

109
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:03,620
nitrate.

110
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:07,860
Swap partners: sodium nitrate is soluble, and barium

111
00:05:07,940 --> 00:05:10,660
sulfate is one of the sulfate exceptions, so

112
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:12,340
it's a white precipitate.

113
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:16,180
Barium ion plus sulfate ion gives barium sulfate

114
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:16,740
solid.

115
00:05:17,220 --> 00:05:20,580
Barium sulfate is so insoluble that hospitals use

116
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:22,980
it in a barium meal: a white drink

117
00:05:23,060 --> 00:05:24,820
that shows up the gut on an X-ray.

118
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:28,740
Dissolved barium ions are poisonous, but solid barium

119
00:05:28,900 --> 00:05:31,700
sulfate barely dissolves, so it passes through the

120
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:33,380
gut without being absorbed.

121
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:36,600
Not every mixture makes a solid.

122
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:40,000
Mix potassium nitrate and sodium chloride.

123
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:43,960
Swap partners: sodium nitrate and potassium chloride.

124
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:47,120
Both soluble, so no precipitate forms.

125
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:50,520
Write "no precipitate", not "no reaction".

126
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:53,320
All four ions are still there, and every

127
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:55,720
one is a spectator, so there's no net

128
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:56,920
ionic equation.

129
00:05:58,134 --> 00:06:00,773
One more question: after you filter off the

130
00:06:00,934 --> 00:06:03,173
solid, what's left in the solution?

131
00:06:03,654 --> 00:06:05,733
The spectators, always.

132
00:06:06,134 --> 00:06:08,613
Plus any ion that was in excess.

133
00:06:08,934 --> 00:06:10,853
Say you add more silver nitrate than the

134
00:06:10,934 --> 00:06:11,973
chloride needs.

135
00:06:12,454 --> 00:06:15,173
The chloride is essentially all used up, so

136
00:06:15,254 --> 00:06:17,893
the extra silver ions have no partner, and

137
00:06:17,974 --> 00:06:19,573
they stay in solution too.

138
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:22,480
So here's the line to remember.

139
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:25,040
Swap partners, check both.

140
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:28,800
Then for the equation: split only the aqueous,

141
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:30,640
and cross out what's the same on both

142
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:31,440
sides.

143
00:06:32,667 --> 00:06:33,306
Your turn.

144
00:06:33,947 --> 00:06:36,426
You mix solutions of copper two sulfate and

145
00:06:36,587 --> 00:06:37,986
sodium hydroxide.

146
00:06:38,267 --> 00:06:40,826
Name the precipitate, and write the net ionic

147
00:06:40,907 --> 00:06:41,546
equation.

148
00:06:42,027 --> 00:06:43,386
Pause here to answer.

149
00:06:53,800 --> 00:06:58,040
Swap partners: copper hydroxide and sodium sulfate.

150
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:00,120
Sodium sulfate is soluble.

151
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:04,280
Hydroxides are mostly insoluble, and copper isn't one

152
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:08,680
of the exceptions, so copper(II) hydroxide precipitates, a

153
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:10,040
pale blue solid.

154
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:13,000
Copper is two plus, so it needs two

155
00:07:13,080 --> 00:07:17,480
hydroxides: copper ion plus two hydroxide ions gives

156
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:19,160
copper hydroxide solid.

157
00:07:19,640 --> 00:07:21,880
If you wrote C u O H, the

158
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:23,400
charges don't balance.

159
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:25,240
Last one.

160
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:28,520
Silver nitrate solution is mixed with more than

161
00:07:28,600 --> 00:07:31,640
enough sodium chloride solution to react with all

162
00:07:31,720 --> 00:07:32,840
the silver ions.

163
00:07:33,320 --> 00:07:35,400
The white solid is filtered off.

164
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:37,640
Which ions are in the filtrate?

165
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:39,480
Pause here to answer.

166
00:07:49,900 --> 00:07:53,340
The filtrate holds sodium ions and nitrate ions,

167
00:07:53,380 --> 00:07:57,100
the spectators, plus the leftover chloride ions, because

168
00:07:57,140 --> 00:07:59,340
the sodium chloride was in excess.

169
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:01,820
The silver ions are essentially all in the

170
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:02,580
solid.

171
00:08:02,860 --> 00:08:05,420
If you listed only sodium and nitrate, you

172
00:08:05,580 --> 00:08:07,020
forgot the excess chloride.

173
00:08:08,234 --> 00:08:11,273
So whenever two ionic solutions are mixed: list

174
00:08:11,314 --> 00:08:14,393
the ions, swap partners, and check both against

175
00:08:14,474 --> 00:08:15,433
the rules.

176
00:08:15,514 --> 00:08:18,073
An insoluble one is the precipitate.

177
00:08:18,234 --> 00:08:20,953
Split only the aqueous compounds, cross out the

178
00:08:21,114 --> 00:08:23,513
spectators, and check the charges.

179
00:08:23,594 --> 00:08:26,633
What stays dissolved is the spectators, plus anything

180
00:08:26,714 --> 00:08:27,913
in excess.

181
00:08:28,114 --> 00:08:30,553
Next: the practical video, where you find these

182
00:08:30,634 --> 00:08:34,233
rules yourself, then Ksp, which puts a number

183
00:08:34,314 --> 00:08:35,353
on "insoluble".

184
00:08:36,567 --> 00:08:38,006
Here are the key notes.

185
00:08:38,247 --> 00:08:40,166
Pause the video if you need more time

186
00:08:40,247 --> 00:08:41,046
to copy them.
